  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "A polynomial $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-x+3.$ Given that $f(3)=0$, what is the sum of the roots of the polynomial?",
  "worked_solution": "If $f(3)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To find the other roots of the polynomial $f(x)$, we need to find the other factors of $f(x).$\n\nSince $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{3} & 4 & -12 & -1 & 3 \\\\  &  & 12 & 0 & -3 \\\\  & 4 & 0 & -1 & \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-x+3}{x-3}=4{x}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-x+3=(x-3)(4{x}^{2}-1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n(x-3)(4{x}^{2}-1)=(x-3)(2x-1)(2x+1).\n$$\n\nNow that our polynomial is fully factored, we can see that the three roots of the polynomial are\n\n$$\nx=3,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the roots is\n\n$$\n3+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=3.\n$$",
